    "description": "Developed distributed data pipelines for data warehousing with Spark, Kafka, and Hadoop to generate reporting data for the Seller Monitoring Experience:\n> Worked with various stakeholders to develop a comprehensive experience for publishers to maintain, monitor, and troubleshoot the performance of digital ad inventory. \n> Includes real-time metrics, batch analytics, forecasting, pacing, and object metadata for inventory sold via: programmatic guaranteed deals, private marketplace deals, direct sold lines, and open auctions. \n> Enables users to efficiently sort, filter, and search across a wide variety of attributes and metrics.\n> Leveraged an existing, generalized reporting API owned by another team.\n\nLead a cross-team initiative to redesign the backend data systems of the Seller and Buyer Monitoring Experiences:\n> Replaced a commercially licensed, hybrid relational and analytical database (SingleStore) by combining open source solutions (Clickhouse and Postgres).\n> Implemented a new API service, including dynamic SQL generation in Java Spring Boot to integrate data from both Clickhouse and Postgres using foreign data wrappers. Optimized for fast query performance with well-designed tables and indexes, common table expressions, and pre-aggregated data wherever possible. \n> Maintained existing API contract to reduce time and effort for consumers to migrate to the new service.\n> Reduced operational overhead by replacing legacy systems and reducing system-wide outages by 100%, saved $500,000 in vendor contract expenses per year, and improved mean API response time by 60%. \n> Provided timely, clear communication with project managers, product managers, product support, and leadership, to maintain transparency in software development, testing, and maintenance processes.\n> Organized the Data Engineering Book Club, building connections across teams, encouraging critical thinking about existing and future systems, and accelerating professional development for all engineers.",
